---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/information-technology/foundational/types-and-components-of-computer-systems">

  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This is the opening lesson of Information Technology, and it assumes you have used a
    computer or a phone — which is all it assumes. Nothing here requires prior computing
    knowledge; every term is defined when it first appears. The lesson opens the machine the
    way every serious ICT syllabus does (Cambridge IGCSE ICT 0417 section 1 is exactly this):
    the parts, the programs, the operating system that connects them, and the machine types
    they get packed into.
  </p>
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>define hardware and software, and classify any given component into the right one;</li>
    <li>walk the fetch–decode–execute cycle and explain the roles of the control unit, the arithmetic and logic unit, RAM and ROM;</li>
    <li>order the storage hierarchy — registers, RAM, SSD, hard disk — and explain why opening an app "loads it into RAM";</li>
    <li>separate applications software from system software, list the operating system's core jobs, and trade off CLI against GUI;</li>
    <li>evaluate the computer types — desktop, laptop, tablet, smartphone — against a given task;</li>
    <li>read the transistor-count curve that explains why your phone exists, and discuss where artificial intelligence and extended reality take it next.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    One idea carries the whole lesson, and we keep returning to it: a division of labour. The
    hardware can only do a short list of primitive things; the software arranges those
    primitives into everything you recognise as "using a computer".
  </p>

  <h2>One idea, repeated everywhere</h2>
  <p>
    Every computer system — from a desktop under a desk to the smartphone in a pocket — is one
    idea repeated: <strong>hardware</strong>, the physical components, running
    <strong>software</strong>, the programs that control the operation of the computer and
    process the data. Nothing else in computing is mysterious once you hold that pairing: the
    processor is the worker, memory is the workbench, storage is the filing cabinet, the
    operating system is the supervisor who decides which job gets the worker next, and the
    applications are the services delivered on top. This lesson walks that stack from the
    bottom up.
  </p>
  <Widget no="3" caption="pair each part of the stack with its job" widget="match" config={ictStackMatch} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> Pair each part with its job and test the metaphor's stretch: the processor is the worker,
    memory the workbench, storage the filing cabinet, and the operating system the
    supervisor deciding which job gets the worker next. Notice what the widget credits the
    software with — arranging a short list of hardware primitives into everything you
    recognise as using a computer.
  </p>
  <p>
    One widening of the frame before we open the box. In the wider world of organisations, what
    we are studying is called an <strong>information system</strong>, and it has six components,
    not two: hardware, software, data, networking communication, people and processes. The
    first four are the technology; the last two — the people who use it and the procedures they
    follow — are what turn the technology into value. A spreadsheet open on a laptop, connected
    to email so a manager can see where the money went, is an information system working: every
    one of the six parts is doing its job at once.
  </p>
  <Widget no="4" caption="file the six components of an information system" widget="sort-buckets" config={ictInfoBuckets} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> File the six components and count the split: hardware, software, data and networking
    communication are the technology, while the people who use it and the procedures they
    follow are what turn the technology into value. Notice the widget's working example — a
    spreadsheet connected to email so a manager can see where the money went is all six at
    once.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> hardware and software are interchangeable labels for
  "physical versus digital things you can see". The test is functional, not visual: does the
  component <em>do</em> the work (hardware) or <em>direct and enable</em> it (software)? A
  printer's driver — software that lets the operating system operate the printer hardware — is
  software even though it once shipped on a disc; a printed page ("hard copy") is hardware
  output.</p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> So far: every system is hardware running software — worker, workbench, filing cabinet,
    supervisor — widened to six components once people and procedures join the frame, since
    they are what turn the technology into value.
  </p>

  <h2>Inside the machine: fetch, decode, execute</h2>
  <p>
    The CPU's job is processing instructions to produce an output, and it does this with a
    three-beat loop — fetch, decode, execute — shared between its two headline parts: the
    <strong>control unit</strong> (CU) that sequences and directs, and the
    <strong>arithmetic and logic unit</strong> (ALU) that computes. The loop's memory partners
    matter as much as the loop itself, and the RAM/ROM split is a guaranteed exam question.
    Walk the whole cycle below, one step at a time.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the fetch–decode–execute cycle, plus the two memories that make it possible — RAM and ROM" widget="equationStep" config={fdeCfg} />

  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> steps 1–3 are the loop itself — the CPU never stops
    running it while powered on, billions of times per second. Steps 4–5 are the two memories
    the loop depends on: RAM as the fast, forgetful workspace the fetch step reads from, ROM as
    the fixed instruction sheet that gets the machine awake enough to load the operating system
    into that workspace in the first place. Notice the dependency chain: ROM wakes the machine,
    the operating system fills RAM, and only then can the cycle have anything useful to fetch.
  </p>
  <p>
    The memory split is worth stating as a contrast you can recite: <strong>RAM</strong> —
    read/write, volatile, holds what is currently in use, contents lost on power-off;
    <strong>ROM</strong> — read-only, non-volatile, holds the bootstrap, contents fixed at
    manufacture. One is a desk you work on; the other is the instructions glued to the inside
    of the machine's lid telling it how to wake up. Everything else the syllabus lists as
    hardware — motherboard, graphics card, sound card, network interface card, storage devices,
    input and output devices — exists to feed the cycle or carry its results. The motherboard is
    the physical joining: buses (address, data, control) running between CPU, memory and the
    slots where expansion cards plug in.
  </p>
  <p>
    The storage hierarchy is worth learning as a speed ladder: CPU registers (fastest,
    smallest) → RAM (fast, volatile) → SSD (fast-ish, permanent) → hard disk (slower, cheap per
    gigabyte, permanent). Each step down buys capacity with latency — which is why opening an
    app "loads it into RAM": dragging the program up the ladder so the CPU can fetch from it at
    full speed.
  </p>
  <Widget no="5" caption="line up the speed ladder, fastest first" widget="order" config={ictLadderOrder} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> Order the ladder fastest-first and read its single trade: CPU registers, then RAM, then SSD,
    then hard disk — each step down buys capacity with latency. Notice what the order
    explains: opening an app loads it into RAM, dragging the program up the ladder so the
    processor can fetch from it at full speed.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> RAM is the same as storage, and "memory" is the same as
  "space". A machine with 8 GB of RAM and a 256 GB SSD has 8 GB of <em>workbench</em> and
  256 GB of <em>filing cabinet</em>. Confusing them leads to the classic wrong diagnosis: a
  full disk is not "out of memory" — though it can still crash a system that uses disk space as
  overflow when RAM fills.</p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> one loop — fetch, decode, execute — plus two memories and a ladder
    of storage. The hardware half of the lesson is complete. Now for the half that tells the
    hardware what to do.
  </p>

  <h2>The software half, and the operating system that runs it</h2>
  <p>
    Software divides into two families. <strong>Applications software</strong> provides the
    services the <em>user</em> requires to solve a task — word processing, spreadsheets,
    databases, video and graphics editing, CAD, web browsers. <strong>System software</strong>
    provides the services the <em>computer</em> requires to operate — operating systems, device
    drivers, utilities, compilers and linkers. The test that separates them: could this program
    exist without a user? A spreadsheet exists for the user; a driver that translates "print
    page 2 in duplex" into signals a specific printer model understands exists for the machine.
  </p>
  <Widget no="6" caption="file each program under its family" widget="sort-buckets" config={ictSoftwareBuckets} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> File each program by its customer: word processors, spreadsheets and browsers serve the
    user, while operating systems, utilities and compilers serve the machine. Notice the
    sharpest card — compilers and linkers, programs almost nobody outside the industry ever
    runs, existing entirely for the machine's benefit.
  </p>
  <p>
    The <strong>operating system</strong> itself is the bridge — the resource manager standing
    between user and machine — and its core jobs can be listed: manage memory, manage files and
    directories, schedule which program gets the CPU, handle input and output, manage security
    and user accounts, and provide the user interface. The families interact in a fixed order:
    the OS starts first (from ROM's bootstrap), loads drivers for the hardware present, and only
    then can applications launch and request services — memory allocation, file access, screen
    drawing, networking — through the OS rather than touching the hardware directly. That
    indirection is why the same application runs on many different machines: it talks to the
    OS's stable interface, not to the metal.
  </p>
  <Widget no="7" caption="find the faulty line — who touches the metal" widget="spot-error" config={ictOsSpot} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> Find the faulty line: applications never touch the hardware directly — they request memory
    allocation, file access, screen drawing and networking through the operating system.
    Notice what the indirection buys, per the correction's logic: the same application runs
    on many different machines because it talks to a stable interface, not to the metal.
  </p>
  <p>
    Operating systems also come in types, matched to the work. <strong>Batch systems</strong>
    run jobs with no interaction at all — a payroll run collects all the calculations and works
    through them overnight. <strong>Time-sharing (multitasking) systems</strong> — your laptop,
    and the Linux and Unix family it descends from — give each open task a tiny slice of CPU
    time called a <em>quantum</em>, then switch; done fast enough, dozens of programs appear to
    run at once. <strong>Distributed systems</strong> spread the work across many interconnected
    machines, each with its own memory and CPU, so files and computation can live anywhere on
    the network. Modern desktop operating systems include the networking stack as a matter of
    course — the descendant of the older specialist "network operating systems" that first let
    personal computers share files and printers.
  </p>
  <Widget no="8" caption="pair each OS type with its behaviour" widget="match" config={ictOsTypesMatch} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> Pair each OS type with its behaviour and find yours on the list: time-sharing gives each
    open task a tiny slice of CPU time called a quantum, which is why a laptop appears to
    run dozens of programs at once. Notice the lineage card too — the older network
    operating systems that first let personal computers share files and printers survive as
    the networking stack inside modern desktop systems.
  </p>
  <p>
    Finally, the OS is the machine's contract with the human. Through a
    <strong>command line interface</strong> (CLI) you type the instructions — terse, scriptable,
    powerful, and still how servers are administered; through a <strong>graphical user
    interface</strong> (GUI) you point and click — discoverable, visual, hungry for screen and
    processing resources. Dialogue-based and gesture-based interfaces (talk to it, swipe it) now
    cover everything from smart speakers to tablets. Same machine, different contract — and each
    interface is a genuine trade-off: the GUI trades expert speed for learnability, the CLI the
    reverse.
  </p>

  <h2>Types of computer: one machine, many packages</h2>
  <p>
    The types of computer in syllabus section 1.4 — desktop, laptop, tablet, smartphone,
    phablet — are not really different machines; they are the same machine at different points
    of a decades-long shrink, and the syllabus wants you to evaluate them against each other.
    The <strong>desktop</strong> trades portability for everything else: cheap expandability
    (standard slots and connectors), the best cooling and hence the best sustained performance,
    easy repair. The <strong>laptop</strong> folds the same architecture into a battery-powered
    clamshell and pays for it in price, repair difficulty and thermal limits. The
    <strong>tablet</strong> and <strong>smartphone</strong> push further into
    <em>portability</em> — instant-on, always-connected, sensor-rich (GPS, accelerometer,
    camera) — and pay in screen size, input precision and upgradeability (usually none). A
    <strong>phablet</strong> blurs the tablet/phone line with a large phone-size screen.
  </p>
  <p>
    The professional skill the section tests is matching type to task: a film editor needs the
    desktop's sustained power; a surveyor in the field needs the phone's portability and GPS; a
    student writing essays sits between them with the laptop. Note also the boundary cases the
    syllabus mentions in passing: mainframe-style servers (the same fetch–decode–execute cycle,
    scaled up in racks) and embedded computers (a washing machine's controller is the same idea
    frozen into one fixed program in ROM).
  </p>
  <Widget no="9" caption="match the machine to the job" widget="branch" config={ictMachineBranch} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> Branch the four briefs and price each trade: the film editor buys sustained power and
    expandability at the cost of portability, the student pays in repair difficulty and
    thermal limits, and the bank's mainframe workloads turn out to be the same
    fetch–decode–execute cycle scaled into racks. Notice the washing machine's card — one
    fixed program in ROM is still a computer by every definition in this lesson.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "a phone is not a real computer." It runs software, it
  fetches, decodes and executes instructions on a CPU with RAM and storage and an operating
  system — it is a computer by every definition in this lesson, merely the most aggressively
  miniaturised package on the list. The same is true of the washing machine's controller: a
  small, single-program computer is still a computer.</p>

  <h2>Why your phone exists: one line on a log plot</h2>
  <p>
    The shrink that produced the smartphone has a quantitative engine, and the plot below is
    it: the number of transistors on a flagship CPU on a log<sub>10</sub> scale — each gridline
    is ten times more transistors. Intel's 4004 of 1971 carried about 2,300 (log<sub>10</sub> ≈
    3.36); the 2022 Apple M1 Ultra carries about 114 billion (log<sub>10</sub> ≈ 11.06). A
    roughly straight line on a log scale is exponential growth — <strong>Moore's law</strong>,
    Gordon Moore's 1965 observation that the component count on integrated circuits was
    doubling roughly every two years — and it is the reason a 1970s computer filled a room that
    today fits in a phone, trading the desktop's expandability for the mobile computer's
    portability.
  </p>
  <Widget no="10" caption="call the multiple — 1971 to 2022" widget="estimate" config={ictMooreEstimate} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> Estimate before revealing: from the 4004's roughly 2,300 transistors to the M1 Ultra's 114
    billion is about fifty million times — and a straight line on a log scale is exactly
    what exponential growth looks like. Notice what the plot's axes are hiding: every equal
    vertical step is a tenfold jump, which is why Gordon Moore's 1965 observation reads as a
    doubling roughly every two years.
  </p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="transistor counts of flagship CPUs, 1971–2022, on a log10 scale — a straight line means exponential growth" widget="plotter" config={mooreCfg} />

  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> the line is close to straight, and on this scale straight
    means explosive — every equal vertical step is a tenfold jump. The distance from the 1993
    Pentium to the 2000 Pentium 4 — seven years on the horizontal axis — represents the
    transistor count multiplying roughly thirteen times. Then look at the last decade of
    points: the slope softens. Growth is continuing, but the doubling has slowed, and that
    flattening is why the "emerging technologies" below matter more than they did a decade ago.
  </p>
  <p>
    The same curve explains price: while a fab (fabrication plant) grows more expensive every
    generation, the cost <em>per transistor</em> kept falling for decades — which is how
    computation became cheap enough to embed in doorbells and trainers.
  </p>

  <h2>Emerging technologies: what the curve buys</h2>
  <p>
    Section 1.5 asks about the impact of emerging technologies, and two headline it. First,
    <strong>artificial intelligence</strong> — machine learning systems trained on examples
    rather than programmed with rules. The transistor budget is what buys it: modern AI runs on
    processors doing arithmetic in parallel at staggering scale, which is why voice assistants,
    translation, image recognition and large language models all arrived in the decade the
    graph's last points cover. Its impact questions are the syllabus's own: which jobs change,
    what happens to privacy when software reads faces, who is responsible when an automated
    decision is wrong. Second, <strong>extended reality</strong> — virtual reality (a fully
    synthetic world, headset and motion tracking) and augmented reality (digital content
    overlaid on the real one, from phone apps to smart glasses). XR is a direct consumer of the
    shrink: it needs two high-resolution displays, a dozen sensors and graphics rendering at
    90+ frames per second, all battery-powered and light enough to wear — a spec sheet that was
    physically impossible in 2005.
  </p>
  <p>
    Both technologies also stress the older parts of the lesson: they demand operating systems
    that schedule real-time sensor data, storage hierarchies that stream gigabytes, and
    interfaces beyond the GUI. The syllabus's request that you discuss their impact on everyday
    life is really a question about the curve above continuing — and, as the flattening of its
    last decade hints, about what happens if it does not.
  </p>

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>A device driver is sold in a box. Hardware or software — and what test did you apply?</li>
    <li>What does the CPU do the instant power arrives, and which memory makes that possible?</li>
    <li>Your laptop slows down with 40 browser tabs open but the disk is nearly empty. Is the problem memory or storage?</li>
    <li>Give one task where a CLI beats a GUI, and one where the reverse holds.</li>
    <li>A surveyor must map a remote hillside. Which computer type fits, and which two properties decide it?</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) Software — apply the functional test: a driver directs and
    enables hardware rather than doing the work itself, and packaging is irrelevant. (2) It
    fetches and executes the bootstrap instructions held in ROM, which wake the machine far
    enough to load the operating system into RAM. (3) Memory: many open tasks compete for the
    fixed RAM workbench, so the OS starts swapping; the empty disk shows storage is not the
    constraint. (4) The CLI wins for scriptable, repetitive server administration; the GUI wins
    for discoverable, visual tasks such as photo editing or a first-time user exploring an OS.
    (5) A smartphone (or tablet with GPS): portability and built-in sensors decide it —
    sustained processing power, the desktop's strength, is not what the task needs.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Hardware does, software directs: the processor is the worker, memory the workbench, storage the filing cabinet, the OS the supervisor.</li>
    <li>The CPU lives on a fetch–decode–execute loop; the control unit directs, the ALU computes.</li>
    <li>RAM: read/write, volatile workspace. ROM: read-only, non-volatile bootstrap. Storage is not memory — the ladder trades speed for capacity.</li>
    <li>Applications serve the user; system software serves the machine; applications talk to the OS's interface, never straight to the metal.</li>
    <li>OS types match the work: batch (no interaction), time-sharing (quantum slices), distributed (many machines, one job).</li>
    <li>Computer types are one architecture at points along a shrink — match type to task, and count the phone as a computer.</li>
    <li>Moore's law — transistor counts doubling roughly every two years — is the engine behind the smartphone, AI and XR; its flattening defines the decade ahead.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    The next lessons go deeper into the software half: how data is organised into files,
    databases and spreadsheets, and how networks connect the six-component information system
    into one working whole. Later units pick up the hardware ladder again — input, output and
    storage devices in their own right — and the safety and security practices that keep
    systems and their people safe.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    Three refinements take you beyond the syllabus summary. First, the fetch–decode–execute
    loop as described is the honest skeleton, but real CPUs overlap it: <em>pipelining</em>
    keeps decode running while the next instruction is fetched and the previous one executes —
    like a laundry line where washer, dryer and folding table all work at once. Add
    <em>multi-core</em> (several complete CPUs on one chip) and the gigahertz number stops
    being the whole story: a 4-core 2 GHz chip outperforms a 1-core 3 GHz chip on almost
    everything modern, because software is built to split across cores. Second, memory
    technology continues to migrate: flash storage (the SSD's technology) began as a way to be
    "RAM that doesn't forget", and modern phones use their fast storage almost as working
    memory — the RAM/ROM/SSD boundaries the syllabus draws are real conceptually but physically
    converging. Third, the historical structure is worth seeing: RAM and ROM's division of
    labour (volatile workspace, fixed bootstrap) descends from the von Neumann architecture of
    1945 — program and data stored together in one memory — which is why the CPU's loop has
    looked structurally the same for eighty years even as its speed improved by factors of
    millions. Moore's law changed the numbers; the architecture is the constant, and that is
    why this lesson's vocabulary — bus, register, interrupt, kernel — will still describe
    machines built long after the current syllabus expires.
  </p>

